y measured procals in about half the  population a level of 0.5 was actually  the strongest predictor for bacterial  infection  but about 20 of those with over 0.5  didn't have a bacterial infection  defined is that because they couldn't  define it and they had one or because  they really didn't it was just bad covid  we don't know  pct of less than 0.1 had a negative  predictive value of  almost 99 for bacterial infection so  again i think  the level is useful if it's really low  probably not  a bacterial infection if it's over 0.5  it  probably is if it's in between it's a  little hard to know what to say because  could it be bad covid  could it be bacterial i think there  you're sort of watching the trend  and trying to make some decisions based  on your testing results  so it's a little tricky uh as the  literature emerged  to know how to interpret it a couple  last comments on implementation and then  i'll go through a couple cases so if you  have procalcitonin  or thinking of implementing it i think  it really helps to have a local expert  somebody that people can ask questions  of  somebody can say hey i got this what do  you think what do you think is going on  whether it's in the lab  pathologist whether that's an id  physician whether that's a stewardship  pharmacist  somebody it's good to develop  recommendations of what to do  and how to use it and how to interpret  it not just put a value out there  that turns red when it's some number but  it's good to like tell people what you  think they should do  because this lab does require a little  bit of interpretation  it's i think good to encourage people to  use it where it has utility  because through track infection sepsis  and don't use it where it doesn't  cystitis localized abscess uti  there's a little literature out there  but i would say it's not ready for prime  time in this area  uh i would look at processes to avoid  inappropriate unnecessary use so we  don't allow people to order daily  procalcitones  um because you know we looked and some  patients had 30 procalcitonins over 30  days which means  no one's doing anything with that data  and so that's just a waste of blood and  a waste of money  and so you want to have some sort of  process to help make sure it's used  appropriately  i think it's useful when you're  presenting the data to not just put it  in your lab  report but to put it where people look  at infection related data we have a  screen where we have fever and white  count and procalcitonin and antibiotics  and culture results  and it's all sort of a one stop shop for  all your infection related data so i  think it's useful to put it there  and then i think having a stewardship  team involved can help with this  in one study where there's a local  stewardship team plus a pcp algorithm  they were able to decrease antibiotic  use by about 20 percent  and then in another study where they  looked at community hospitals where they  had pct available  and compared to community hospitals  where they didn't they saw  decreased length of stay and a  non-significant decrease in the body use  was 1.5 days  and they shortened had a shorter length  of stain now is that all from  procalcitonin  hard to tell those places with pcb also  had pharmacist-driven algorithms  so there is suggestion that you know  combining these with stewardship  really is probably the uh the best way  to do this as it is with like bra  all the rapid diagnostics for infection  so key points the dynamics are very  important and very useful  you want to keep in mind the confounders  when interpreting you want to use them  in syndromes where they have data  loris retract infection sepsis don't use  them where they don't have data  don't use them if you're not going to  use it right why get a lab you're going  to ignore  and so don't bother getting it if you're  not going to do anything with it  if you and that's what i always tell my  residents if the test will not  change your treatment today why are we  doing it  um and then usually we don't recommend  testing more than every two to three  days unless you're in an ambiguous  situation we're like well  i want to do one it's low i don't think  needing antibiotics but i'm kind of  nervous  i'll do one in six to 12 hours and if  that's negative i'll feel really  comfortable that we're not giving  anybody  so with that i will stop and go through  a couple of cases all these cases are  people that i've seen  so 65 year old female recently  discharged from the hospital  had a acute mi got a stead she comes in  with shortness of breath over the last  day  she's a cough productive of a little bit  of sputum  hypoxic in her facility no beaver chills  she does have leukocytosis  mild elevation lactic acid mild acute  kidney injury chest x-ray shows interval  left  right left greater than right new  opacities especially the bases  is it edema is it an infection uh so the  question is really is this sepsis  without a lactic acid  an acute kidney injury which needs  antibiotics and you know 30 ml per kilo  or is this heart failure with some renal  hypoperfusion  lord pro cal was .0 less than 0.05  and so the ed gave her a dose of  antibiotics but the medicine team did  not  and her ef was like 30 percent and she  had nuance at a-fib  and they gave her a bunch of diuretics  and by the next day she was way better  so zelsan and 30 mls per kilo would not  be the way to treat this patient and the  pro calcium is useful  for helping us sort that out 77 year old  female  with dementia o2 dependency opd presents  from long-term care for this altered  mental status increased o2 need  coverage been in the facility she's got  crackles on exams she's febrile or white  counts eight  she's got biohazy opacities maybe  developing consolidations or pct's 0.13  so this is a little iffy to me this  sounds a lot like coven  i wouldn't generally start antibiotics  but if i didn't i'd repeat a procal in 6  to 12 hours  if you 