ank you now um next  question  how is pct cleared does renal impairment  have an effect on decreasing levels  yeah it can have some effect uh it is  cleared somewhat  renally maybe some through dialysis and  that's where it can be a little  difficult to interpret in some of those  icu patients  um and so but again really low  useful high useful it's that sort of .25  to one ish  one and a half can be a little hard to  interpret  but generally i've interpreted it you  know as i normally would  keeping those sort of nuances in there  and nobody really knows the answers to  how much or when or  that just as far as i know isn't  available there's a nice review of the  literature that's been published in  clinical infectious disease  a couple years ago addressing this topic  sort of summarizing  how to interpret pro-calcium into the  setting of renal dysfunction  okay great thank you now among the  physicians utilizing  pct today in the us what percentage  would you say are familiar with the  cutoff values and recommended actions  for each of the cutoffs oh that that and  that's the difficult part right  it depends by center how how well people  understand it  and that's where it's it's actually even  difficult when you bring it on in your  lab in the jabbed up discussion  which value am i am i going to flag is  abnormal am i going to flag the 0.25 or  am i going to flag the 0.5  is it used for sepsis does it use for  larger tract infection because  you know the lab doesn't know which is  being used for so we flag it at anything  over 0.1  because point one is sort of where you  might think about it maybe in your bad  cp exacerbations  um but it you know it's highly variable  and my  my sense is that nuance is sometimes  lost on people  okay thank you dr van you have time for  just a few more questions here  now you have very very nicely stated  that pct is a tool  for the hospitalist now what about the  em practitioner  emergency medicine that's so what i  would say is 